Terminusmod provides a true bold for the popular terminus 12 pt font, a modified version of the 12 pt with bold, and versions with status icons.

The versions with status icons are the ascii sets with some "icons" I drew to use in status bars.

There are 6 fonts included:

terminusmod12b.pcf (bold to be used with terminus 12)
terminusmod12r.icons.pcf (terminus 12 with status icons)
terminusmodx12r.pcf (modded version of terminus 12)
terminusmodx12b.pcf (modded bold version of terminus 12)
terminusmodx12r.icons.pcf (modded version of terminus 12 with status icons)
terminusmodx12r.psfu (consolefont for terminusmodx)
ISO8859-1 only for now.

You can use something like this for your .Xresources:

urxvt*font:         -*-terminus-medium-r-normal-*-12-*-*-*-*-*-*-*
urxvt*boldFont:     -*-terminusmod-bold-r-normal-*-12-*-*-*-*-*-*-*

To use the consolefont for terminusmodx, do:

#setfont terminusmodx12r

See your distro's documentation for a more permanent solution.

The full names of the .pcf fonts are:
-misc-terminusmod-bold-r-normal-*-12-120-72-72-c-60-iso8859-1
-misc-terminusmod.icons-medium-r-normal-*-12-120-72-72-c-60-iso8859-1 
-misc-terminusmodx-medium-r-normal-*-12-120-72-72-c-60-iso8859-1
-misc-terminusmodx-bold-r-normal-*-12-120-72-72-c-60-iso8859-1
-misc-terminusmodx.icons-medium-r-normal-*-12-120-72-72-c-60-iso8859-1

To install manually, copy the .pcf files to /usr/share/fonts/local and copy the .psfu files to /usr/share/kbd/consolefonts.

Then, do:

$fc-cache -f
$mkfontscale /usr/share/fonts/local
$mkfontdir /usr/share/fonts/local

Add this to your .xinitrc if you don't have it already:
xset +fp /usr/share/fonts/local
xset fp rehash

You can view the font with xorg-xfd or xorg-xfontsel.

You can also view and modify the font with gbdfed (http://sofia.nmsu.edu/~mleisher/Software/gbdfed/). You'll need bdftopcf and pcf2bdf.
First, convert the .pcf file to .bdf (#pcf2bdf -o terminusmod12r.icons.bdf terminusmod12r.icons.pcf).
Next, open the bdf file with gbdfed and edit to suit.
Then, convert it back to .pcf (#bdftopcf -o terminusmod12r.icons.pcf terminusmod12r.bdf).
Reinstall it.

To use the icons, (from a terminal) you can do something like (#echo -e '\xC9') to display a clock for example, and then copy the output to a script.

The inspiration for the icons came from lokaltog (https://github.com/Lokaltog) and his post in the dwm hackers thread (https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?pid=724236#p724236) on Arch Linux Forums.

A better solution for icons (in dwm at least) might be to use stlarch_font (http://sourceforge.net/projects/stlarchfont/).
It's a font that only contains icons and uses the Unicode Private Use area. See the README.stlarch for more info.

Created by stlarch (stlarchie@gmail.com)

